      "role": "hook",
      "headline": "Chapter 3 finale: two thousand years of Christianity on one timeline.",
      "body": "From a Jewish teacher in Galilee to churches on every continent. Lay out the turning points, then see how the three great branches fit together."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.religions.030.c2",
      "type": "timeline",
      "headline": "How Christianity took shape",
      "events": [
        {
          "when": "c. 30 CE",
          "label": "Jesus crucified in Jerusalem under Pontius Pilate"
        },
        {
          "when": "c. 70 CE",
          "label": "Mark writes the first Gospel"
        },
        {
          "when": "325",
          "label": "Nicaea: the Son 'of one substance' with the Father"
        },
        {
          "when": "367",
          "label": "Athanasius lists the 27 New Testament books"
        },
        {
          "when": "1054",
          "label": "Schism separates East and West"
        },
        {
          "when": "1517",
          "label": "Luther's Ninety-five Theses"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "card.religions.030.c3",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Three branches, one story",
      "body": "In 2010, Pew estimated, about half of the world's Christians were Catholic, 37% Protestant in the broad sense and 12% Orthodox. All three accept the Nicene Creed. They part over authority: the pope for Catholics, self-governing churches for the Orthodox, and for Luther, scripture alone."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.religions.030.c4",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Common ground",
      "body": "Across the branches, the building blocks are familiar. Baptism with water admits new members, whether as infants or as professing believers. Communion recalls the Last Supper and is the central act of worship in most churches. And the year still turns on Easter, the first Sunday after the first full moon on or after March 21."
    },
